  "Number": 35,
  "Question": "With a rational denominator, the expression \\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3-\\sqrt5}\\) is equivalent to:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{3+\\sqrt6+\\sqrt{15}}6\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt6-2+\\sqrt{10}}6\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{2+\\sqrt6+\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{2+\\sqrt6-\\sqrt{10}}6\\)",
    "E": "None of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Group \\(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3\\) together. Since \\(2+3=5\\), a difference of squares will cancel the rational terms in the denominator. Also,\n\\[\n(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3)^2=5+2\\sqrt6>5,\n\\]\nso \\(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3>\\sqrt5\\), and the original denominator is positive.\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by the positive quantity \\(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3+\\sqrt5\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt2}{\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3-\\sqrt5}\n=\\frac{2+\\sqrt6+\\sqrt{10}}\n{(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3)^2-5}\n=\\frac{2+\\sqrt6+\\sqrt{10}}{2\\sqrt6}.\n\\]\nOnly one radical remains in the denominator. Multiplying numerator and denominator by \\(\\sqrt6\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\sqrt6+6+\\sqrt{60}}{12}\n=\\frac{2\\sqrt6+6+2\\sqrt{15}}{12}\n=\\boxed{\\frac{3+\\sqrt6+\\sqrt{15}}6}.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice A.",
